  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSurface high pressure across the area will provide mostly dry \nconditions to the region. There will be some slight chances for \ndiurnal showers and thunderstorms albeit isolated in nature for\nTuesday although this should be confined to the northern tiers \nof our County Warning Area. According to the National Blend of \nModels Wednesday and Thursday looks to be dry although one \ncannot rule out a diurnal shower or thunderstorm during the \nmaximum daytime heating as the area gets to anomalously high \ntemperatures for this time of year. Also, the moisture flux \nfrom the Great Lakes driving around the periphery of the high \npressure system will traverse into the area which will bring \nhigher dew points, therefore humid conditions along with the \nheat will be the main theme through Thursday.\n\nThis moisture flux track could advect in more cloud coverage \nand shower potential then expected, therefore the confidence on\nPoPs is low as central guidance is having a hard time dealing \nwith this solution and setup. For Tuesday, heat indexes were \nborderline or less for advisory criteria, therefore no headlines\nwere issued as of now. For Wednesday and Thursday, that may be \na different story as indexes exceed advisory criteria across \nmuch of the lowlands, especially across the Tri-state and \nCharleston Metro areas. This will have to be closely monitored \nfor the next forecast package or two. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nFor Thursday, shower potential rises as a cold front swings \ndown from the south and promotes shower and thunderstorm \nactivity out ahead of the frontal boundary. SPC has us already \nin a slight chance or 15% for possible severe thunderstorm \npotential with modest shear and instability to support this as \nwell as anomalously high temperatures. For Friday, the cold \nfrontal boundary slides through promoting more chances for \nthunderstorm activity as well as severe potential as SPC has us \nin a slight 15% probability as well for the afternoon and\nevening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nModels have surface high pressure building in behind the front \nwhich should suppress much in the way of shower or thunderstorm \npotential. Drier weather is expected and cooler weather will \nbreak the warming trend for the weekend. The area will likely \nbecome settled after Saturday as mid and upper level heights \nbuild slowly behind the aforementioned cold frontal boundary. \nMid to upper level high pressure will reinforce settled weather \ngoing into the new work week.",
